ltsp6-session-tuning
====================

What this package changes, and - more usefully - what it refuses to change.

The three settings
------------------

1. XDG_CACHE_HOME moves to /var/cache/ltsp6-sessions/<uid>

   ~/.cache is the busiest directory a desktop session owns: fontconfig
   caches, GTK icon caches, thumbnails, browser caches. With home
   directories on NFS every write goes over the network, once per session,
   and none of it is worth keeping between logins. Moving it to local disk
   is the single largest improvement in this package.

   The cache root is sticky and world-writable, like /tmp. The Xsession hook
   therefore tests "-O" - do we own the directory - before using it. If
   another user pre-created a directory for our uid, the test fails and the
   session quietly keeps the default ~/.cache. Degraded, never exploited.

   Consequence worth knowing: a user's cache does not follow them between
   application servers. That is correct for a cache, and it is why the load
   balancer's session affinity matters for feel rather than function.

2. xfwm4 compositing off by default

   In an xrdp session Xorg runs on llvmpipe - software rendering. Every
   shadow, fade and transparent panel is then composited by the CPU, once
   per session, and none of it survives RDP compression in a way a user
   would notice. A user who wants it back can turn it on in Window Manager
   Tweaks and keeps their own setting.

3. inotify headroom and vfs_cache_pressure

   The inotify limits are per-uid, so they are not shared between sessions -
   but one Xfce session spends them freely (xfsettingsd, xfdesktop, Thunar,
   panel plugins, gvfs, tumbler, dconf, then the user's own applications).
   Exhaustion surfaces as EMFILE from inotify_init, usually printed as "too
   many open files", which sends people looking at file descriptor limits
   instead. Headroom costs a few kilobytes per instance.

   vm.vfs_cache_pressure = 50 halves how eagerly the kernel drops dentry and
   inode caches. With NFS homes each dropped entry becomes a network round
   trip on the next lookup, and desktops do a great many small lookups. If
   your home directories are local, the default of 100 is fine and this line
   can go.


What was deliberately left out
------------------------------

Each of these appears in most "multi-user tuning" advice. They were measured
on Debian 13 before being rejected, so nobody has to spend an afternoon
re-adding them.

  * UserTasksMax / DefaultTasksMax
    systemd ships /usr/lib/systemd/system/user-.slice.d/10-defaults.conf with
    TasksMax=33%, and Debian sets kernel.pid_max = 4194304 in
    /usr/lib/sysctl.d. That is roughly 1.38 million tasks per user. It is not
    the limit anyone is hitting.

  * kernel.pid_max
    Already 4194304 from Debian's own sysctl.d. Raising it again changes
    nothing.

  * vm.max_map_count
    Already 1048576 from Debian's own sysctl.d.

  * nofile limits
    systemd's default soft limit is generous for a desktop session and the
    hard limit is 1048576. Raising the soft limit globally mostly helps
    programs that allocate by the limit rather than by need.

  * vm.swappiness
    A genuine trade-off in both directions, not an improvement. Lowering it
    keeps idle sessions resident, which helps a returning user - but it also
    makes the kernel drop page cache instead, and with NFS homes that cache
    is expensive to refill. Left at the default on purpose; tune it against
    your own memory pressure, not against advice.


Thumbnails on network home directories
--------------------------------------

Moving the cache off NFS stops tumbler *writing* thumbnails over the network,
but it still *reads* the files to make them. On a host with many sessions and
large media in home directories that read load can be worth stopping.

This package does not ship it, because /etc/xdg/tumbler/tumbler.rc is a
conffile belonging to tumbler-common and a package must not fight another
package for a file. Apply it locally instead - the key names below are from
that shipped file:

    # /etc/xdg/tumbler/tumbler.rc
    [FfmpegThumbnailer]
    Excludes=/home
    [GstThumbnailer]
    Excludes=/home
    [RawThumbnailer]
    Excludes=/home
    [PopplerThumbnailer]
    MaxFileSize=8388608

"Excludes" takes a ;-separated path list and, per tumbler's own
documentation, takes precedence over "Locations". The other sections are
JPEGThumbnailer, PixbufThumbnailer, CoverThumbnailer, FontThumbnailer,
OdfThumbnailer, EpubThumbnailer and DesktopThumbnailer.


Checking that it took effect
----------------------------

    # in a session, after logging in again:
    echo "$XDG_CACHE_HOME"            # -> /var/cache/ltsp6-sessions/<uid>
    xfconf-query -c xfwm4 -p /general/use_compositing   # -> false
    sysctl fs.inotify.max_user_instances vm.vfs_cache_pressure

The Xsession hook only runs for sessions started after installation. Existing
sessions keep the old environment until the user logs out and back in.
